**Which version and why:** answer honestly. A sensible answer is C++17, or C++11/14 if your course uses it, with the reason: wide compiler support, features such as smart pointers, move semantics, `auto` and lambdas. Show that you know what newer versions add.

**C++14 vs C++20 (key differences):**
- **C++14** was a small refinement of C++11: generic lambdas, return type deduction for functions, variable templates, `std::make_unique`, relaxed `constexpr`, binary literals and digit separators.
- **C++20** was a major release: **concepts** (constraints on templates), **ranges** (composable views and algorithms), **coroutines**, **modules**, the three-way comparison operator `<=>`, `std::span`, `consteval`, designated initializers and `std::format`.

**Binary search (iterative):**

```cpp
int bs(const vector<int>& a, int key) {
    int lo = 0, hi = (int)a.size() - 1;
    while (lo <= hi) {
        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;   // avoids overflow
        if (a[mid] == key) return mid;
        if (a[mid] < key) lo = mid + 1; else hi = mid - 1;
    }
    return -1;
}
```

**Recursive:** same logic with `bs(a, key, lo, hi)` that returns `-1` when `lo > hi` and recurses on one half. It uses O(log n) stack.

**With iterators (generic):**

```cpp
template <class It, class T>
It bsearch_it(It first, It last, const T& key) {
    It end = last;
    while (first < last) {
        It mid = first + (last - first) / 2;
        if (*mid < key) first = mid + 1; else last = mid;
    }
    return (first != end && !(key < *first)) ? first : end;
}
```

This works for random-access iterators. The standard library offers `std::lower_bound` and `std::binary_search`.

**Complexity:** binary search is O(log n) on sorted data. Linear search is O(n).

**Iterators:** objects that act like pointers to traverse containers (`begin()`, `end()`, `*it`, `++it`). Categories: input, output, forward, bidirectional, random access (and contiguous in C++17/20). **Range:** a pair `[begin, end)` of iterators in the traditional model. In C++20, a range is anything with `begin()` and `end()`, so you can write `std::ranges::sort(v)`.
